# TODO before first release: point at the dedicated paulikit
# repository once it exists. This currently names the research
# monorepo the package is being extracted from, which is the wrong
# target for a citation.
repository-code: "https://codeberg.org/mkhellat/openqcp-lab"
abstract: >-
  paulikit computes the exact Pauli decomposition of an arbitrary
  complex matrix via the fast Walsh-Hadamard transform. Hermitian
  input is an optional fast path (real coefficients, with the
  Hermiticity assumption checked rather than assumed), not a
  restriction: general non-Hermitian matrices are fully supported and
  are verified against an independent projection oracle and against
  PennyLane.
  The transform itself is established practice; the contribution here
  is making it usable at scale - a streaming formulation whose peak
  resident memory is bounded independently of the term count, a
  chunk-framed binary checkpoint format cheap enough to leave
  permanently enabled, cache-aware automatic chunk sizing, and
  exhaustive per-term verification against an independent projection
  oracle at up to 91,652,096 terms.
keywords:
  - Pauli decomposition
  - fast Walsh-Hadamard transform
  - quantum Hamiltonian simulation
  - linear combination of unitaries
  - memory-bounded algorithms
  - scientific Python
  - checkpoint and restart
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
version: 0.1.0
